     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. A confirmation without a clinical rationale doesn't change my concern. 100 mg is 10 times the maximum daily dose of 10 mg. For an adult with normal kidney and liver function and no other unusual factors, I can't identify any indication that would justify it. The risk of serious harm is high, and a single dose is hard to reverse because amlodipine's half-life is 30 to 50 hours.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** I can enter 10 mg (or 5 mg, or 2.5 mg) by mouth once daily right now.\n- **Verify with pharmacy or your attending.** If a unit of measure was mixed up or a protocol calls for something unusual, they can confirm. If they approve 100 mg, it should be entered through their process with documented justification.\n- **Check for a different problem.** If this is for an overdose or a different drug, tell me the details and I can help you work out the right order.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
